--TEST--
a capability a package lists as `optional` compiles where its namespace's `[grants]` line does not list it, the call throws a catchable `RuntimeError` when it is reached, and `Core\Cap::has` returns `false` for it in that package — `rule:security/optional-capability-degrades`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: tools:packages/grants-what-each-namespace-may-do
autoload 'Acme\Disk' from './vendor/acme/disk/src';

echo Acme\Disk\Store::save("note"), "\n";
try {
    Acme\Disk\Store::force("note");
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
echo Acme\Disk\Store::reads() ? "reads" : "no reads", "\n";
--FILE vendor/acme/disk/src/Store.nvs--
<?nvs
namespace Acme\Disk;

final class Store
{
    // The package lists `fs.write` as optional, and the grant line does not
    // list it. So `Core\Cap::has` returns `false` here.
    public static function save(string $text): string
    {
        if (Core\Cap::has("fs.write")) {
            Core\IO::write("note.txt", $text);
            return "disk";
        }
        return "memory";
    }

    // This call compiles. It throws a `RuntimeError` when it runs.
    public static function force(string $text): void
    {
        Core\IO::write("note.txt", $text);
        echo "not reached\n";
    }

    // The grant line lists `fs.read`, so this is what the server allows.
    public static function reads(): bool
    {
        return Core\Cap::has("fs.read");
    }
}
--FILE vendor/acme/disk/package.toml--
[package]
name = 'Acme\Disk'

[capabilities]
required = ["fs.read"]
optional = ["fs.write"]
--FILE package.toml--
[grants]
'Acme\Disk\*' = ["fs.read"]
--EXPECTF--
memory
`Core\IO::write` needs `fs.write`. The namespace `Acme\Disk` does not have it. Add "fs.write" to the line 'Acme\Disk\*' in `%s` to allow it.
reads
